WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.550
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Welcome
to Deconstructing Chatbots.

00:00:02.550 --> 00:00:03.840
I'm Priyanka Vergadia.

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:05.520
And in this episode,
we will learn

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:08.580
how to integrate a Dialogflow
agent with a back end

00:00:08.580 --> 00:00:11.385
system like a database,
calendar, or a CRM.

00:00:11.385 --> 00:00:14.640
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:19.100 --> 00:00:22.600
Any chatbot you build will
need to connect to a back end

00:00:22.600 --> 00:00:26.260
to transfer information to
and from the business logic.

00:00:26.260 --> 00:00:29.770
Within Dialogflow, we are able
to make that connection using

00:00:29.770 --> 00:00:31.210
fulfillment.

00:00:31.210 --> 00:00:34.840
Fulfillment is a piece of code
which is deployed as a web hook

00:00:34.840 --> 00:00:37.780
and lets your Dialogflow
agent call the business

00:00:37.780 --> 00:00:40.930
logic on a per intent basis.

00:00:40.930 --> 00:00:43.420
During a conversation,
this allows

00:00:43.420 --> 00:00:47.080
you to use the information
extracted from Dialogflow's

00:00:47.080 --> 00:00:51.070
natural language processing
to generate a dynamic response

00:00:51.070 --> 00:00:54.700
or to trigger action
on your back end.

00:00:54.700 --> 00:00:57.010
Let's take our appointment
scheduler chatbot

00:00:57.010 --> 00:00:59.980
from previous episodes and
implement a fulfillment

00:00:59.980 --> 00:01:02.150
for scheduling appointments.

00:01:02.150 --> 00:01:05.620
Well, currently, our agent
identifies the user's intent

00:01:05.620 --> 00:01:07.750
of scheduling an
appointment and provides

00:01:07.750 --> 00:01:11.560
a static response indicating
that the appointment is set up.

00:01:11.560 --> 00:01:13.960
Now, in a real
world scenario, we

00:01:13.960 --> 00:01:16.360
need to set up that
appointment on a calendar

00:01:16.360 --> 00:01:19.120
and also check for any
scheduling conflicts, which

00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:22.100
requires us to integrate
with the calendar.

00:01:22.100 --> 00:01:24.610
So let's jump into
our Dialogflow console

00:01:24.610 --> 00:01:26.710
and see how it is all set up.

00:01:26.710 --> 00:01:31.180
Click on the gear icon near your
agent name to open Settings.

00:01:31.180 --> 00:01:33.370
Look at the Google
Cloud Project ID

00:01:33.370 --> 00:01:37.640
and click on it to open
the project in GCP console.

00:01:37.640 --> 00:01:40.090
Navigate to the
hamburger menu icon

00:01:40.090 --> 00:01:41.950
and locate APIs and Services.

00:01:44.530 --> 00:01:47.230
Click on Enable
APIs and Services

00:01:47.230 --> 00:01:50.540
and look for Google
Calendar API.

00:01:50.540 --> 00:01:54.910
Once you find it, enable the
API on your cloud project.

00:01:54.910 --> 00:01:59.210
Now under the same APIs and
Services within Credentials,

00:01:59.210 --> 00:02:01.690
click on Create
credentials dropdown

00:02:01.690 --> 00:02:04.240
to select Service account key.

00:02:04.240 --> 00:02:06.220
Under Create
service account key,

00:02:06.220 --> 00:02:09.130
select the New service
account from the dropdown

00:02:09.130 --> 00:02:11.330
and enter an account name.

00:02:11.330 --> 00:02:15.240
Make sure JSON is the key
type and then click Create.

00:02:15.240 --> 00:02:18.200
In the popup, select
Create without role.

00:02:18.200 --> 00:02:21.160
A JSON file will be
downloaded to your computer.

00:02:21.160 --> 00:02:25.460
And you will need this later,
so take note of its location.

00:02:25.460 --> 00:02:28.760
Before we close it out
though, copy the client email.

00:02:33.420 --> 00:02:35.250
Now open Google Calendar.

00:02:35.250 --> 00:02:39.750
And on the left, click the plus
sign and select New Calendar.

00:02:39.750 --> 00:02:42.900
Enter AppointmentCalendar
for the name of the calendar

00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:45.490
and select Create Calendar.

00:02:45.490 --> 00:02:47.410
Next, go to the
AppointmentCalendar

00:02:47.410 --> 00:02:49.510
that will appear on the left.

00:02:49.510 --> 00:02:52.060
Click Add People
and paste the email

00:02:52.060 --> 00:02:54.680
copied from that JSON file.

00:02:54.680 --> 00:02:57.840
Then select Make changes
to events in the Permission

00:02:57.840 --> 00:03:01.330
dropdown and click Send.

00:03:01.330 --> 00:03:04.840
At this point, we are ready to
go back to our Dialogflow agent

00:03:04.840 --> 00:03:06.610
and set the fulfillment.

00:03:06.610 --> 00:03:09.460
Click on Fulfillment and
enable the inline editor.

00:03:11.868 --> 00:03:13.410
Check out the link
in the description

00:03:13.410 --> 00:03:18.210
below to get the required code
for package.json and index.js.

00:03:18.210 --> 00:03:21.960
Open package.json file and
copy and paste the dependencies

00:03:21.960 --> 00:03:23.730
into fulfillment.

00:03:23.730 --> 00:03:25.960
Do the same for
index.js as well.

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:35.810
Grab the Calendar ID
from the Google Calendar.

00:03:35.810 --> 00:03:38.030
We just create it and
replace that in index.js.

00:03:41.420 --> 00:03:44.660
Next, copy the contents of
the JSON file downloaded

00:03:44.660 --> 00:03:46.820
in the Service
Account Setup section

00:03:46.820 --> 00:03:50.820
and paste it into the empty
object, ServiceAccount.

00:03:50.820 --> 00:03:52.670
Check out the rest of the code.

00:03:52.670 --> 00:03:55.640
Make appointment function
contains the responses

00:03:55.640 --> 00:03:57.140
to the user.

00:03:57.140 --> 00:03:59.510
And create calendar
event function

00:03:59.510 --> 00:04:02.720
takes care of grabbing the
entities like date, time,

00:04:02.720 --> 00:04:06.470
and appointment type, and
creates the calendar invite.

00:04:06.470 --> 00:04:08.810
It also looks for the
calendar conflicts

00:04:08.810 --> 00:04:11.180
and throws exceptions.

00:04:11.180 --> 00:04:14.910
Once you're done, click Deploy
at the bottom of the page.

00:04:14.910 --> 00:04:19.190
Now, we have one more thing to
do before we can start testing.

00:04:19.190 --> 00:04:22.880
Click on Intents and go to the
Schedule Appointment intent.

00:04:22.880 --> 00:04:27.670
Scroll down to Fulfillment
and enable webhook and save.

00:04:27.670 --> 00:04:29.230
Now we're ready to test.

00:04:29.230 --> 00:04:32.740
In the simulator on the right,
query your Dialogflow agent

00:04:32.740 --> 00:04:35.260
with something like, set
an appointment at 4:00

00:04:35.260 --> 00:04:36.580
PM tomorrow.

00:04:36.580 --> 00:04:39.580
And then respond to
the follow up queries.

00:04:39.580 --> 00:04:41.980
After getting the
required information,

00:04:41.980 --> 00:04:44.320
you will see that the
appointment is added

00:04:44.320 --> 00:04:46.870
to the appointment calendar.

00:04:46.870 --> 00:04:50.320
You can verify that if you
ask for another appointment

00:04:50.320 --> 00:04:53.680
at the same time slot, your
agent will respond back

00:04:53.680 --> 00:04:57.090
with an appropriate message
since that slot is not free.

00:04:59.650 --> 00:05:00.150
All right.

00:05:00.150 --> 00:05:01.430
So that was a lot.

00:05:01.430 --> 00:05:03.660
Let's summarize what
we learned today.

00:05:03.660 --> 00:05:06.080
We enhanced our appointment
scheduler chatbot

00:05:06.080 --> 00:05:09.710
by creating a fulfillment
through inline editor,

00:05:09.710 --> 00:05:13.670
enabled Google Calendar
API for the GCP project

00:05:13.670 --> 00:05:16.760
and used those APIs
credentials to connect

00:05:16.760 --> 00:05:20.480
our fulfillment Cloud
Function with Google Calendar.

00:05:20.480 --> 00:05:22.700
And finally, we
were able to test

00:05:22.700 --> 00:05:24.860
our agent by
creating appointment

00:05:24.860 --> 00:05:27.110
request on the calendar.

00:05:27.110 --> 00:05:30.290
Don't miss the next episode
of Deconstructing Chatbots

00:05:30.290 --> 00:05:33.140
to learn more about
conversational AI.

00:05:33.140 --> 00:05:36.290
Please like and subscribe to our
channel for more such content.

00:05:36.290 --> 00:05:39.940
[MUSIC PLAYING]

